if you can think in a systematic way and think of these basic constructs like you know Q's and Q mean I mean Q's in a system, things like arrival rates, things like service rates,

182
00:18:36,870 --> 00:18:39,990
things like if my node can process so many requests, what happens over there?

183
00:18:39,990 --> 00:18:46,470
They found that these candidates would get better at thinking at incrementally higher level of granularity.

184
00:18:46,470 --> 00:18:56,710
So design rounds became more and more about the concepts of building ambiguous systems that may not even be real but could stress your thinking on a very vendor agnostic way.

185
00:18:56,720 --> 00:19:08,549
So I can talk about when I failed my first design interview at at Google 2008 and this is when I saw one of these questions for the first time and last 10 15 years this has been a staple for student design interviews.

186
00:19:08,549 --> 00:19:18,311
You might see the depth and scope and expectations gotten higher in the last few years, but this structure has been fairly consistent. I would say 2010 onwards.

187
00:19:18,320 --> 00:19:22,950
You said just now when you failed your first system design interview at Google.

188
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:24,789
I'd love to hear that story.

189
00:19:24,799 --> 00:19:27,868
There's nothing quite like exposing yourself on live radio, is there?

190
00:19:27,878 --> 00:19:34,871
[laughter] Well, actually, so my first design interview that I failed was in 2008. Sadly, he wasn't my last.

191
00:19:34,871 --> 00:19:38,950
I would continue to fail them for another I think under five or so years.

192
00:19:38,950 --> 00:19:40,789
Sadly, without even knowing why I was failing.

193
00:19:40,799 --> 00:19:45,750
The way I would fail them and later I would see that was a pretty common thing.

194
00:19:45,750 --> 00:19:54,549
I mean I wasn't unique there was that I would go into a Sims interview and somebody would ask me you know design X and I would suddenly throw things like caches

195
00:19:54,549 --> 00:20:03,510
and components and technologies on the board without understanding what the problem was about, what are the requirements, what are the behavioral expectations or the performance expectations of a system.

196
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:09,430
Actually, the first one I was ever asked was, you had to push 100 terabytes of data into a data center.

197
00:20:09,430 --> 00:20:11,830
How would you do this?

198
00:20:11,830 --> 00:20:15,669
Looking back now, you break it down and you can do it in a series of steps.

199
00:20:15,669 --> 00:20:17,909
But I just came in with, well, I would use FTP.

200
00:20:17,909 --> 00:20:20,470
That was my vague response, right?

201
00:20:20,470 --> 00:20:23,590
And that opened up a whole rabbit hole of, well, what about this, what about that, what about that?

202
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:26,630
It became a pretty much a catamask game where I was playing catchup.

203
00:20:26,630 --> 00:20:30,070
And I would do this for many years without knowing why I would fail the interviews, right?

204
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:34,790
I mean, I would come out of this thinking that, hey, I had all the right buzzwords. I had FTP in there.

205
00:20:34,790 --> 00:20:36,311
I had pipes in there. I had switches in there.

206
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:37,909
I had everything in there.

207
00:20:37,909 --> 00:20:41,750
But I still failed.

208
00:20:41,750 --> 00:20:43,990
Around 2016, I was at an Uber interview which forever changed my life.

209
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:49,190
The question was designing Twitter and same thing.

210
00:20:49,190 --> 00:20:54,950
I was throwing around things like cues and load balancers and nodes and Dynamo DBs and whatnot.

211
00:20:54,950 --> 00:21:01,029
I was struggling and the interviewer I think had a pained look on his face.

212
00:21:01,029 --> 00:21:03,110
So in a very rare act of generosity,

213
00:21:03,110 --> 00:21:12,950
he kind of asked me one question and the question was what would be the first request that would fail and that was towards the end of the interview and I packed up and I went home.

214
00:21:12,950 --> 00:21:16,230
Weirdly enough when I went home I ran across this book here.

215
00:21:16,230 --> 00:21:24,071
I'm pointing to a book called database management systems by Ragu Ramakrishna and Gerke. It's a book on databases.

216
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:27,510
More specifically, it's a book on database internals.

217
00:21:27,510 --> 00:21:32,230
And what it talks about is how do indexes work in a database? What kind of latencies they give you?

218
00:21:32,230 --> 00:21:34,710
What kind of response times they give you?

219
00:21:34,710 --> 00:21:41,909
So that question made me look at this book and realize well what if I had asked how many requests will this system be getting?

220
00:21:41,909 --> 00:21:44,710
If you got to dump it all into database, when would the first failure be?

221
00:21:44,710 --> 00:21:48,471
The first failure would be when the database cannot handle load. When can it not handle a load?

222
00:21:48,471 --> 00:21:50,310
And then work backwards.

223
00:21:50,310 --> 00:21:59,830
Suddenly, I don't know if you played that game, uh, the incredible machine back in the '9s, you would build these Rube Goldberg machines where you light a candle, the candle blows a balloon,

224
00:21:59,830 --> 00:22:06,870
the balloon flows up and turns switch on and the switch turns on a fan which hits a cat, some weird machine, right?

225
00:22:06,870 --> 00:22:13,190
And you kind of think of that and you work backwards and each of these components becomes part of that larger system.

226
00:22:13,190 --> 00:22:17,590
So, this interview kind of made me realize, okay, if I can break my problem down into these steps and I'll talk about those steps too, right?

227
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:26,789
like ask for the requirements look at exactly what are the SLOs's if I need to put something in Twitter as a user how much delay am

228
00:22:26,789 --> 00:22:41,270
I allowed to tolerate as a feed consumer am I okay if I see a post from you taking more than 10 seconds more than 5 seconds more than 20 seconds like where would I be happy about so putting these numbers in place like kind of helped me learn about okay what if I design a system

229
00:22:41,270 --> 00:22:49,750
that matches these different criterias so that was kind of the aha moment I guess on how I started learning about it I would still fail But at least now I know why I was failing.

230
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:53,029
Yeah.

231
00:22:53,029 --> 00:22:57,430
So you've talked about failures learning from failure and how you've evolved your approach.

232
00:22:57,430 --> 00:23:02,870
Is there any key takeaways or lessons learned from that process that you haven't covered?

233
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:11,750
I know that Uber failure and recovery seems like a rocky style montage over the weekend, but reality was that aha moment was pure luck.

234
00:23:11,750 --> 00:23:13,750
I happened to have that book at at home and I found it.

235
00:23:13,750 --> 00:23:21,270
But what I did thankfully do was reach out to a few of my friends and pitch to them my thoughts of why had gone wrong. At that time my network was pretty small.

236
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:24,310
So I had to kind of figure out on my own.

237
00:23:24,310 --> 00:23:31,110
So the friends who had I mean I mean my colleagues who are very very helpful they started challenging a few of my assumptions on what I thought these design interviews were like.

238
00:23:31,110 --> 00:23:41,590
So as I was doing more mocks you know getting their sense of how I was doing going from complete confusion on their faces to this growing clarity on how I was performing right helped me kind of formulate my own way.

239
00:23:41,590 --> 00:23:43,430
And again, it's not a patent approach or anything.

240
00:23:43,430 --> 00:23:53,750
What helped me at the time was really breaking the problem down into a gathering all requirements, you know, functional requirements, non-functional requirements, and even extensions that you might want to consider.

241
00:23:53,750 --> 00:23:59,909
And then really going down and to talk about if you were to solve those requirements, how do you build the APIs or what would the APIs look like?

242
00:23:59,909 --> 00:24:05,990
What would be the entities and objects in your system you would store for serving those API those requirements?

243
00:24:05,990 --> 00:24:13,350
and then get to a point where you can draw on the board a very very high level diagram of system that is purely just functional and not scalable.

244
00:24:13,350 --> 00:24:16,630
And if if that can take care of all your functional requirements in the first five or 10 minutes,

245
00:24:16,630 --> 00:24:27,350
you are like a long way in because that sets the stage for a showing that you thought about them on day one and b cut down any ambiguity on where the interviewer might want to you know trick you with.

246
00:24:27,350 --> 00:24:30,630
And now is when you look at the non-functional requirements.

247
00:24:30,630 --> 00:24:32,549
Things like read should take no more than 10 millconds.

248
00:24:32,549 --> 00:24:34,630
Rise should not take more than 200 millconds.

249
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:37,190
Freshness SLOs's and so on.

250
00:24:37,190 --> 00:24:40,549
Now is the system that you have in place with one database and one server for example.

251
00:24:40,549 --> 00:24:44,789
Can it scale? Well, obviously can't. So how do we do this? Can you where can you scale?

252
00:24:44,789 --> 00:24:51,590
Where can you break the bottlenecks and systematically evolve grow each branch of that design tree one piece at a time?

253
00:24:51,590 --> 00:24:57,909
So I think this structured thinking or this structured way kind of helped me break the problem down.

254
00:24:57,909 --> 00:25:01,110
And for me the back of the envelope math was something that I was usually good at.

255
00:25:01,110 --> 00:25:04,950
So that helped me kind of you know turn the problem to my like into space that I was strong at.

256
00:25:04,950 --> 00:25:13,590
So if I can take a problem put the numbers on various bits of expectations on your system then I can extend each one of those to see what solves it and what trade-offs I got to make.

257
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:19,350
How do you go about communicating the tradeoffs effectively?

258
00:25:19,350 --> 00:25:27,909
So given my strings are actually being quantitative showing that if I know a database for example can do a read in a millisecond it can do a write in 5 millconds

259
00:25:27,909 --> 00:25:36,470
for example I can use that to show that if I use this technology with these guarantees I would get these SLOs's that I'm meeting

260
00:25:36,470 --> 00:25:49,669
but the cost of doing that might be literally high cost for instance you might say that putting everything in memory or in a very very highly performant database can get you set lows but if your customers don't care or if your users don't care and if they're very cost sensitive.

261
00:25:49,669 --> 00:25:52,470
You might be okay with a low SLO in part system.

262
00:25:52,480 --> 00:25:55,750
So showing this with numbers is I think the easiest way to do it.

263
00:25:55,750 --> 00:25:57,430
It's also the most intuitive way as well.

264
00:25:57,430 --> 00:25:59,029
So numbers speak for themselves.

265
00:25:59,029 --> 00:26:02,149
You can show how one part can affect something else and what might be the rule for trade-off.

266
00:26:02,159 --> 00:26:06,310
Now I want to switch tracks considerably.

267
00:26:06,310 --> 00:26:10,310
We've been talking about the structure of the interviews and what the candidate faces.

268
00:26:10,310 --> 00:26:15,110
I want to look at it now from the interviewer's perspective.

269
00:26:15,110 --> 00:26:27,751
In a company, is the interviewer given instructions like do a system design interview or do companies pretty much standardize so everyone gets as close as possible to the same interview? The answer is yes to both.

270
00:26:27,760 --> 00:26:32,390
Typically, what happens when a company lacks a strong interviewer bench?

271
00:26:32,390 --> 00:26:36,710
They might be putting all their eggs on a single like one or two very senior engineers in the company.

272
00:26:36,710 --> 00:26:47,190
If you think of a startup, that person is usually a CTO and that CTO is most likely going to be using their set of problems that they have or they might have in the next 3 to 6 months

273
00:26:47,190 --> 00:26:51,750
and use that as a basis for throwing problems at the candidate which is not a bad thing because you're testing for practical applications.

274
00:26:51,750 --> 00:27:01,750
As companies grow, what happens is there is a bigger emphasis on being fair, not being biased, and ensuring that all candidates have a,

275
00:27:01,750 --> 00:27:10,149
you know, fairly similar interview bar and experience without it being a thing that you can look off Reddit and learn the answer for.

276
00:27:10,149 --> 00:27:18,149
So, companies actually invest in interviewer training so that they can remove some of the bias language, they can be more focused, they can actually be more targeted towards the candidates's experience.

277
00:27:18,149 --> 00:27:27,510
You don't want a front-end engineer asking a very fairly heavily front- end focused question for a candidate who's probably a backend engineer or a platform engineer or in engineer and vice versa.

278
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:33,669
So, companies invest in both training and domain based interviewing skills and interviewing coaching, right?

279
00:27:33,669 --> 00:27:35,510
They also do this for leveling as well, right?

280
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:41,350
They want to make sure that everybody at a certain level has similar bar and being is being assessed in a similar way.

281
00:27:41,350 --> 00:27:43,269
So, they can remove some of the bias around how these interviews happen.

282
00:27:43,279 --> 00:27:51,029
You also have panels that look for how a interview went along with the transcript.

283
00:27:51,029 --> 00:28:02,630
By the way, interviewers write down these interviews typically and present it back to a committee or a panel to review both the candidate performance as well as see if there are any anomalies on how the interviewer kind of you know might have gone too much in the one side.

284
00:28:02,630 --> 00:28:16,310
I've seen interviewers where interviewers might come in at large companies with a very very very focused domain specific question that could throw off a candidate on a completely different domain because the candidate is spending time trying to understand the domain and not focusing on the design.

285
00:28:16,310 --> 00:28:23,110
And I've seen companies give feedback to the interviewer and bring the candidate back in for a second round because the first round was way off course.

286
00:28:23,110 --> 00:28:24,630
They happen both ways.

287
00:28:24,630 --> 00:28:29,669
I mean they're both good in some some sense and they both have their downsides.

288
00:28:29,669 --> 00:28:35,350
Interviewers adaptively scale the level of difficulty based on how the candidate's doing.

289
00:28:35,350 --> 00:28:43,350
If they solve the easy problem, then throw some new requirements in or increase the scale by 10.

290
00:28:43,350 --> 00:28:48,470
Something to constantly challenge the candidate. Again, happen depends on the culture. Before I talk about that, let me talk about the leveling first.

291
00:28:48,480 --> 00:28:55,110
So, the difficulty of questions when they start off with are also dependent on the target level of the candidate.

292
00:28:55,110 --> 00:29:04,230
a candidate who's being interviewed for L4 or or mid-level you know engineering roles will be looked more for the breadth do they know about the different technologies

293
00:29:04,230 --> 00:29:13,190
for example do they know about API gateways do they know about you know a cache are they using it and so on if the candidate is acing that if let's say if an L4 or a mid-level candidate is acing

294
00:29:13,190 --> 00:29:22,149
that they might start going deeper right well you mentioned you can use a load balancer okay tell me about how that could perform in this scenario

295
00:29:22,149 --> 00:29:31,430
now as they get senior the the it becomes more deeper you know they're starting to look for candidates, you know, senior candidates to have more advanced design skills, you know, knowing when to use what kind of storage systems,

296
00:29:31,430 --> 00:29:37,510
reason about performance, benefits, SLOs's, and so on, and really be able to articulate their own design choices.

297
00:29:37,510 --> 00:29:42,950
Here's where you'll find Canada shift from, well, I read that in a book to I've seen that being used, so I can empathize with it.

298
00:29:42,950 --> 00:29:57,669
And then as they get you a more you know senior like staff senior staff like well you really want people who can who have built the stuff from ground up and brought in their technical choices and have lived through it and have you know fixed issues with it and scaled it and so on.

299
00:29:57,669 --> 00:30:02,310
So it's not so much that a interviewer will make it challenging for the sake of it,

300
00:30:02,310 --> 00:30:07,590
but often it's to see how far the candidate can go so they can probe their depth and their breadth

301
00:30:07,590 --> 00:30:18,070
and really look at other aspects beyond just is the candidate answering my questions versus is the candidate owning and driving the interview process and really treating me as a peer.

302
00:30:18,070 --> 00:30:21,830
And obviously at any point in time both the interviewer and or the candidate may reach the limit.

303
00:30:21,830 --> 00:30:27,430
But the interviewer usually has the advantage of having gone through this before and preparing the question. The interviewer knows the question beforehand.

304
00:30:27,430 --> 00:30:32,310
So they usually have a leg up because well they know the various parts in that tree.

305
00:30:32,320 --> 00:30:40,389
Taking into account leveling, fairness, uniformity, the different levels of people you get.

306
00:30:40,389 --> 00:30:43,669
What is a reasonable approach to giving candidate hints?

307
00:30:43,679 --> 00:30:45,990
This is also is very non-standard.

308
00:30:45,990 --> 00:30:54,230
I've seen companies and interviewers within companies penalize candidates for even a single hint and I've seen you know companies

309
00:30:54,230 --> 00:31:02,549
and interviewers who provide the context around how the how and why the hint was given and then there's you know how well the candidate takes the hint takes the feedback

310
00:31:02,549 --> 00:31:11,430
and course corrects I was in an interview recently where the question was around I believe it was around I think it was my favorite about Uber as well and there was a point

311
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:19,430
where the candidate was stuck I don't want to give too much detail because he might if that person is listening to this I don't want uh you know give it away. So at this point I mean this guy was doing really well by the way.

312
00:31:19,430 --> 00:31:29,430
So towards the end I wanted to kind of push in one area and asked question around can you make some trade-off that is unconventional in how you would make this better

313
00:31:29,430 --> 00:31:42,070
and the candidate paused for a few seconds and he wasn't quite sure where to go and then I give a simple hint hey what if you want to think of that right and suddenly the candidate just exploded in a good way he just had that epiphany and he was able to kind of explain it and roll with it.

314
00:31:42,070 --> 00:31:44,870
Is that a bad candidate?

315
00:31:44,870 --> 00:31:49,350
No, because that candidate clearly knew about this whole area.

316
00:31:49,350 --> 00:31:51,909
It just hadn't made a connection in that interview pressure.

317
00:31:51,909 --> 00:31:58,230
So I personally will look for things like that and give the candidate the benefit of the doubt.

318
00:31:58,230 --> 00:32:01,269
But there is no standard forgiveness procedure there. So be ready for anything.

319
00:32:01,279 --> 00:32:06,470
Earlier you talked about learning from failures as a candidate.

320
00:32:06,470 --> 00:32:12,549
What about mistakes that interviewers make either that you've made or that you've seen?

321
00:32:12,549 --> 00:32:19,430
So it's an interesting question and sadly not devoid of a non-controversial answer.

322
00:32:19,430 --> 00:32:28,310
What I mean is a lot of times interviewers do not know that they are not providing a fair interview experience.

323
00:32:28,310 --> 00:32:37,509
you know there are biases and it goes both ways and interviewers might feel that hey look I know this area I'll make sure that this area is so stripped down

324
00:32:37,509 --> 00:32:51,909
so it becomes fair but it might not be the case and also given the market and so on it it really depends on what kind of safety and inclusiveness measures the company and the interviewer are operating under for that feedback to a be discovered by the committee

325
00:32:51,909 --> 00:33:03,830
or somebody and then b for that to get back to the interviewer and then there is no really set process in how that retraining, recaching happens for that to not make it way again.

326
00:33:03,830 --> 00:33:07,669
The closest I've seen is companies might blacklist certain questions.

327
00:33:07,669 --> 00:33:17,669
They might change some of the language that interviewers are supposed to use in the feedback process, but it's not a clear standard around this is how you should do this and this how you should not do this.

328
00:33:17,669 --> 00:33:20,870
It happens, but there's no set hard and fast rule.

329
00:33:20,870 --> 00:33:25,990
A lot of the times to mitigate this, companies pair up an interviewer with a shadow.

330
00:33:25,990 --> 00:33:31,509
So the shadow can also record notes and offer you know feedback if necessary or where possible.

331
00:33:31,519 --> 00:33:35,830
Sometimes the shadow and the interviewer leveling difference is quite high.

332
00:33:35,830 --> 00:33:39,430
So you might not see the feedback getting there.

333
00:33:39,430 --> 00:33:45,350
So one way I would mitigate this is by having shadows who are at the same level and who don't have a conflicting kind of reporting structure.

334
00:33:45,350 --> 00:33:50,789
So the feedback can flow and can be taken into account.

335
00:33:50,789 --> 00:33:53,990
But really as you know it is a fairly I guess cloudy process.

336
00:33:53,990 --> 00:34:03,909
Is there any story that you could share where you were the interviewer you asked a question and either at the time or later you think I could have done that better?

337
00:34:03,919 --> 00:34:15,909
So in my early days of interviewing I had this and this is both coding and for design interviews right I had gone in with the mental and this is like you know 15 something years ago I would go in

338
00:34:15,909 --> 00:34:26,470
with the mentality of I'm the interviewer I need to show with the bosses and again it's a really really embarrassing at best and something to be ashamed of at worst.

339
00:34:26,470 --> 00:34:31,510
Your role as an interviewer is not a superiority contest, right?

340
00:34:31,510 --> 00:34:33,030
It is to attract the best candidates for your company, right?

341
00:34:33,040 --> 00:34:43,109
Or for your team, for your business, like somebody you want to work with, somebody who will lift your game, which is almost a way of saying they need to be better than you, but you need to be, you know,

342
00:34:43,109 --> 00:34:48,069
testing them for that, but also be humble enough to highlight and accept it.

343
00:34:48,069 --> 00:34:54,310
And many interviewers early in their interviewing kind of career, they go in with that look, I can't look bad, you know, in that setting.

344
00:34:54,310 --> 00:35:00,550
And a lot of great companies kind of help you with that coaching, help you with pairing up you with the right level of people.

345
00:35:00,550 --> 00:35:05,589
So that like your growth as interviewer is also organic and over time.

346
00:35:05,589 --> 00:35:07,430
So this room for this ego clash doesn't come in.

347
00:35:07,430 --> 00:35:15,910
Now if I was to go back to those initial set of interviewers that I had been very mean and harsh on, I would definitely definitely, you know, slap myself on the back of the head and uh coach myself.

348
00:35:15,910 --> 00:35:19,190
But I'm hoping that this question and the, you know, podcast does a better job.

349
00:35:19,200 --> 00:35:21,671
Yeah. Well, that's a a great lesson.

350
00:35:21,680 --> 00:35:26,710
Now, switching back to the candidate or this could be from either side.

351
00:35:26,710 --> 00:35:41,589
You've talked a lot about there's an intentional ambiguity and interviewers are looking for a candidate to add some structure that's not there as far as what they talk about, how they talk about it.

352
00:35:41,589 --> 00:35:44,470
Do you have any guidelines as far as time management?

353
00:35:44,470 --> 00:35:50,790
Primarily be on the candidate side, but it could be on either side of you've got 40 minutes.

354
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:54,870
Well, let me talk about that from both sides from the candidate side.

355
00:35:54,870 --> 00:35:57,349
So, first of all, the sections as a candidate that I want to kind of cover, right?

356
00:35:57,349 --> 00:36:08,710
I want to cover the requirements, the API and entities, the high level design and then scalability barriers and then extensions or towards the end some variation of that.

357
00:36:08,710 --> 00:36:11,109
And when I practice for interviews and I still have to because it's something that you have to do, right?

358
00:36:11,119 --> 00:36:12,630
And if you don't do it, you lose it.

359
00:36:12,630 --> 00:36:23,589
So, I typically try to cover both the requirements, the APIs and entities in the first 5 to 10 minutes max. And then high level design where just things just work without any skill concerns.

360
00:36:23,589 --> 00:36:27,109
Another 5 minutes and that includes just drawing something on the board, right?

361
00:36:27,109 --> 00:36:31,190
Or Excal or Mirror or whatever online tool we use.

362
00:36:31,190 --> 00:36:45,109
Then that leaves you what 5 10 15 minutes in a 45minut interview if my math is correct about 20 minutes for the rest which is not bad because you can actually talk about and you find that in all these design interviews you know where the scalability barriers are going to be.

363
00:36:45,109 --> 00:36:53,190
So as you prepare beforehand you know that okay the first thing they're going to ask me scale goes number of users goes up by 10x 100x thousandx what do

364
00:36:53,190 --> 00:37:02,069
we do so this becomes a very structured learning process and I would say focus on that but now on the other side you might be thinking oh this is easy as an interviewer if I see a candidate like this perfect

365
00:37:02,069 --> 00:37:13,349
right now in all this time what you want to do is you want to drive the conversation so that you aren't just plowing forward but giving interviewer enough room to you know what I'm going to go with this API because of these assumptions I do that.

366
00:37:13,359 --> 00:37:18,470
If that was the case, which way do you want to go? If you don't say anything, I'll go with this. I just want to make sure that you're okay with it.

367
00:37:18,470 --> 00:37:25,910
So, here's where you're kind of having that explicit silent contract with the interviewer that your path is kind of, you know, it's a dual commitment.

368
00:37:25,910 --> 00:37:37,589
And then as you do this throughout the process, that gives less opportunity for kind of trick questions as in any trick questions or backing up has to be explicit and an exception rather than the rule.

369
00:37:37,589 --> 00:37:42,710
Now, on the interviewer side, you want to kind of give enough time to follow a candidate on their pace.

370
00:37:42,710 --> 00:37:47,430
And I usually have the candidate walk through a certain line of reasoning to see where they're going with it, right?

371
00:37:47,430 --> 00:37:51,430
See if I can if I can tie them back to the scalability barriers later on.

372
00:37:51,430 --> 00:38:03,190
I usually avoid interrupting unless I see that the interview's completely going off track and you know it's completely off topic or at a path where there's no way they can come back to the scale concerns and scalability concerns later on.

373
00:38:03,190 --> 00:38:11,589
So depending on the interviewer I mean on your personality you might be somebody who has control and be upfront about look I want to hear about requirements I want

374
00:38:11,589 --> 00:38:19,990
to hear about high level design I want to hear about all these can you go through it you can do that or if you're an interviewer who's bit more free flowing you can choose you can you let the candidate drive it

375
00:38:19,990 --> 00:38:29,030
but then pull the reinss in if you think it's not going well this process you're talking about where you're getting in dialogue and negotiation

376
00:38:29,030 --> 00:38:36,950
with the interviewer and I'm saying if I'm the candidate you're saying okay I got this and I could go this way or that way and do you want me to talk about the APIs or security?

377
00:38:36,960 --> 00:38:47,589
You're answering one of those questions that they didn't ask but it's really important which is are you reasonable person to work with and that you can negotiate in the workplace.

378
00:38:47,599 --> 00:38:49,349
Yes, you're right.

379
00:38:49,349 --> 00:38:53,991
You know they are looking for that but sometimes they're not or sometimes they don't know that they should be looking for it. Right.

380
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:59,670
I've been on the receiving end of interview where the question was literally design system design Uber.

381
00:38:59,670 --> 00:39:04,550
If I have concerns, I will raise my eyebrow, right?

382
00:39:04,550 --> 00:39:10,230
And so as a candidate, you're pretty much looking for those eyebrow raises that may be there or you might have missed it.

383
00:39:10,230 --> 00:39:14,069
So you do find both sides and and some interviewers may not even value for it. So you got to read the room.

384
00:39:14,069 --> 00:39:17,910
You got to see what the interviewers like and you get that from experience.

385
00:39:17,910 --> 00:39:20,710
And even now I sometimes misread it. So it is just a matter of practice.

386
00:39:20,720 --> 00:39:25,430
The way I interpret that example is the interviewing process.

387
00:39:25,430 --> 00:39:30,550
It's a two-way street and the company is being interviewed as well.

388
00:39:30,550 --> 00:39:37,190
And if I go into a workplace where their mode of communication is raised eyebrows, I want a bit more direct communication.

389
00:39:37,190 --> 00:39:39,109
So I probably don't want to work there.

390
00:39:39,109 --> 00:39:41,910
So it's not that that's the mode of communication.

391
00:39:41,910 --> 00:39:43,750
It's probably that particular interviewer, right?

392
00:39:43,750 --> 00:39:54,150
I mean, I've been in many panels where you would find a whole range of interviewer personalities or a single interviewer may have different different moods depending on the day, right?

393
00:39:54,150 --> 00:39:58,230
So, it's hard to kind of know what mood that person is in or what, you know, which interview you're getting that day.

394
00:39:58,240 --> 00:40:08,790
Again, I'm not absolving them of any responsibility, but it's hard to paint an entire company in that light or in that scene because of one odd interview.

395
00:40:08,800 --> 00:40:10,870
Right.

396
00:40:10,870 --> 00:40:15,910
Again, it's really an end by matrix where all companies have all kind of personas and you're the outsider.

397
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:21,109
We have one question I think would be great to close out.

398
00:40:21,109 --> 00:40:27,190
This was also suggested by you and I also like it because I have no idea what I'm going to get.

399
00:40:27,190 --> 00:40:30,550
What are some common misconceptions about the system design interview?

400
00:40:30,560 --> 00:40:33,589
So, two things, two of many.

401
00:40:33,589 --> 00:40:47,270
A that you have to cover the highest scale that there is in a system with zero errors with zero cost of operations that there is a perfect solution that covers every angle in a system.

402
00:40:47,270 --> 00:40:55,910
candidates tend to forget that there are trade-offs and trade-offs are actually the highlights of a system design and not things you avoid.

403
00:40:55,910 --> 00:40:57,589
The other one is this may not be a misconception but it's a trap right?

404
00:40:57,599 --> 00:41:01,990
Many candidates end up going for complexity.

405
00:41:01,990 --> 00:41:15,670
They think that their system needs to have highly scalable like in a lot of Kafka cues and many many microservices and you name it like name the flavor of the month and it's got to be there and they end up over complicating a system when a simple system would do.

406
00:41:15,670 --> 00:41:21,430
So choosing complexity to show you know skill is one of the traps.

407
00:41:21,430 --> 00:41:24,390
The other one is being focused on technology rather than you know systems.

408
00:41:24,400 --> 00:41:32,470
In fact after that Uber interview every interview of mine I had never focused on technology.

409
00:41:32,470 --> 00:41:37,991
I would focus on concepts like if my database had this kind of index that would help me with this kind of SLO. Any vendor you want that's fine.

410
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:48,309
In fact, I would only use estim at the end where I would go look because I need a thousand hosts today's EC2 prices, EC2 instance prices are I don't know I think it's $7 for a mini or whatnot, right?

411
00:41:48,319 --> 00:41:50,230
This is what it's going to cost you.

412
00:41:50,230 --> 00:41:52,390
So based on that cost, this is your capex.

413
00:41:52,400 --> 00:41:53,990
What can you do?

414
00:41:53,990 --> 00:42:01,190
And I would say one other misconception is that people think a system design interview is purely purely technical.

415
00:42:01,190 --> 00:42:06,309
It is by and large a communication and time and expectations management interview.

416
00:42:06,309 --> 00:42:15,510
I think we've covered that a few times, but was there anything way you wanted to expand or elaborate on that last point that we haven't?

417
00:42:15,520 --> 00:42:19,030
Yeah. So, if I was to add something new to it, I mean, think about this.

418
00:42:19,030 --> 00:42:27,910
As I said before, there are 30 problems in the system design interview problem set, right?

419
00:42:27,910 --> 00:42:32,950
Like 30 or so problems like there's Uber, Bitly, Dropbox, Ticket Master, Tinder, like there's a few standard problems, right?

420
00:42:32,950 --> 00:42:34,550
And still people struggle with it.

421
00:42:34,550 --> 00:42:43,670
I mean how hard double quotes how hard can it be to master 30 problems but if you think about it people fail or people struggle because they look at it as I need to go there draw a bunch of boxes

422
00:42:43,670 --> 00:42:53,270
and it's all an aha moment from there but imagine the last product that was built it was not built because somebody decided to throw in boxes it was built because there was a user need

423
00:42:53,270 --> 00:43:07,190
that they had to understand there was a business requirement or business need that they had to solve and they had constraints they didn't have oodles of funding on day one they had to go with three boxes and then incrementally make improvements even if they were bad ones because they were the right ones at that point in time like

424
00:43:07,200 --> 00:43:15,349
that doesn't happen in isolation it happens through communication it happens through leadership it happens through explaining and articulating what you're doing why you're doing and you know when

425
00:43:15,359 --> 00:43:25,589
you can come back to it so as a senior engineer a lot of your time goes into articulating all these rather than just writing code or building systems and I think being good at

426
00:43:25,589 --> 00:43:36,390
that articulation and the non-technical aspect helps you do well in these interviews too Sam I think that's a great place to wrap up to close out the show.

427
00:43:36,390 --> 00:43:42,230
Is there any place you want people to find you on the internet or anything you're involved with?

428
00:43:42,240 --> 00:43:47,910
I talk about system design problems on my own blog buildage.com.

429
00:43:47,910 --> 00:43:58,790
I also did a talk in open-source summit North America in Denver in July 6 months ago where I built a tool for system design, I guess visualization.

430
00:43:58,790 --> 00:44:03,829
It was just a fun tool for learning how to reason about systems. you might find it useful or I would love to collaborate.

431
00:44:03,839 --> 00:44:06,309
Sounds good, sir.

432
